Update Manager — platform launchers
===================================

The app itself is one cross-platform file. Everywhere you can simply run:

    python updatemanager.py        (or python3 updatemanager.py)

These launchers are optional double-click conveniences. Each one is clearly
labelled in its header with the operating system it is dedicated to. They all
invoke the same updatemanager.py and auto-activate the virtualenv if present
(the program's own env/ first, then a shared ../env).

Each OS has a NORMAL launcher (no console, GUI only) and a DEBUG launcher
(opens a console and shows all output/errors; pauses at the end). The window
and launchers use the app icon (Favicon.ico on Windows, Logo.png elsewhere).

  linux/
    updatemanager.sh            Normal launcher (no terminal).
    UpdateManager.desktop       Application-menu entry — GUI only (Terminal=false),
                                icon = img/Logo.png. Replace {INSTALL_DIR} and copy
                                to ~/.local/share/applications.
    updatemanager-debug.sh      Debug launcher — runs in the terminal, shows errors.
    UpdateManager-Debug.desktop Debug menu entry — opens a terminal (Terminal=true).

  macos/
    UpdateManager.command       Normal launcher (double-click). First run:
                                chmod +x UpdateManager.command
    UpdateManager-Debug.command Debug launcher — opens Terminal, shows errors.

  windows/
    UpdateManager.vbs           Fully silent launcher (no console) — recommended.
    UpdateManager.bat           Command-Prompt launcher (no console window).
    UpdateManager-Debug.bat     Debug launcher — keeps the console open, shows errors.
    CreateShortcut.vbs          Creates a Desktop shortcut to UpdateManager.vbs with
                                Favicon.ico as its icon (double-click once).
